<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1964 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="zh">大海航行靠舵手</bdi> / <bdi lang="zh-Latn">Dàhǎi hángxíng kào duòshǒu</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A short Chinese song of the 1960s praising the ideas of Mao Zedong, sung over and over by Red Guards.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Yu Wen (Li Yuwen)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Wang Shuangyin</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Chinese Communist Party and the People's Republic of China</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese (Mandarin)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Harbin, China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>spring 1964</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Yu Wen (Li Yuwen), music by Wang Shuangyin, 1964)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a>&quot;, sometimes called &quot;The Helmsman Sets the Ocean Course&quot; in English, is a revolutionary song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. It was written in the spring of 1964 by two artists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> Opera House, the composer Wang Shuangyin and the lyricist Yu Wen (Li Yuwen), at a time of a nationwide campaign to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s writings; its first title was &quot;Making Revolution Depends on Mao Zedong Thought&quot;. Wang Shuangyin sang it at the second Harbin Summer music festival, where it caused a stir.</p><p>In June 1964 he sang it at an evening reception in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> for the North Korean leader Choe Yong-gon. Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> met him afterwards, praised the song and suggested syncopating a few notes, and the authors revised it accordingly. On the advice of Wang Jingzhi, deputy head of the arts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> Television (the forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Central Television), the song took its present title. Its melody closely resembles the first four lines of an earlier song, &quot;I Dedicate Oil to My Motherland&quot;, composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongcheng">Yongcheng</a> with words by Xue Zhuguo; because of the charge of plagiarism, Wang Shuangyin apologised to Qin Yongcheng in person.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> (1966–1976) the song was sung widely by the public, and especially by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a>, in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought and the party. Short and easy to sing, its single verse was usually repeated over and over. Its title phrase appeared on the cover of a junior middle school textbook on Mao Zedong Thought in the 1960s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> used the song's words in an inscription; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>, the title came from a slogan of Lin Biao. The song was also popular in the Third World.</p><p>Its success brought its composer office: Wang Shuangyin became a deputy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a> provincial party committee, head of the province's model opera troupe and deputy director of its culture bureau, and was received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a> fell in 1976 he was detained for investigation, stripped of all his posts and expelled from the party, and questioned about his ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> and Jiang Qing; his membership was restored in 1987. On 24 October 1976, at a rally celebrating the fall of the Gang of Four, the new party chairman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a> led the other leaders onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> rostrum to the song, which for a short while served to praise him and was used in newsreels and documentary films.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A short song in praise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> Thought and the Communist Party, presenting them as the helmsman that guides the revolution; brisk and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
